{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to eb56ba90, CE-2: yes, the one scenario as you propose.\n\nThe scenario runs the real command dispatch and\n`applicationCommands`, and each invocation opens the CLI files and\nthe disk custody again. That covers what a second process adds for\nthis rule: the record is on disk and not in memory. The spawned CLI\nwitness that exists stays. Add no second spawned case.\n\nTwo conditions.\n1. The scenario reaches the proof through the calls that the real\n   command makes, in their order. That includes the request for\n   the read session. The scripted transport answers that route. Do\n   not hand the command a session object from outside.\n2. The note names what the scenario does not run: a new process,\n   and the session authority of a Native Worker. One sentence for\n   each.\n\nThe five steps stand: the lost original install, the failed proof\nat an earlier valid head, the judged Rules checkpoint at the\nexpiry, the proved `unsent`, and the terminal repeat after a\nreopen. Add no test helper beyond `installGraph` and\n`judgeCheckpoint`.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}